async function addToCart(store: CartStore, item: CartItem) {
  // Optimistic: render the item immediately.
  store.setItems([...store.items, item]);

  try {
    await api.post('/cart/items', {
      sku: item.sku,
      quantity: item.quantity
    });
  } catch (error) {
    logger.error('add to cart failed', { sku: item.sku, error });
  }
}

referenceAnswer:
An optimistic update is a two-sided bet and this code only implements the winning side. Snapshot before the write, restore on failure, and surface the failure to the user:

```ts
async function addToCart(store: CartStore, item: CartItem) {
  const snapshot = store.items;
  store.setItems([...snapshot, item]);

  try {
    const server = await api.post('/cart/items', { sku: item.sku, quantity: item.quantity });
    store.setItems(server.items); // reconcile with the authoritative list
  } catch (error) {
    store.setItems(snapshot);
    notify.error('Could not add that item');
    logger.error('add to cart failed', { sku: item.sku, error });
  }
}
```

The snapshot has to be taken before the optimistic write, and the reconcile step matters as much as the rollback: the server may have clamped the quantity or repriced the line, so trusting the local guess on success is the same class of bug one step later.
